# The lifetimes module (spec §19.2, §5.5, §16): what the std's natives hold and keep, which no code
# shows the analysis of lifetimes, and `Holder`, which keeps values for as long as it lives. The two
# annotations stand only on the std's declarations, since a host keeps no language values (§17.3).
#
# A `Holder` keeps each value inside a closure capturing it, so one holder keeps values of any type
# with no `any` type, and the analysis of lifetimes sees what it keeps through its closures: a value
# reaching its own holder is the cycle error (§16), as with any container. It is generic over the mark
# of what it keeps (§9.2), as `Deferred` is, since a closure keeping an ista future is ista code.

# On an extern struct: a value of it holds what a value of `T` holds, a tuple listing several types
# and `()` none. Without it, a value holds its type arguments.
struct Holds<T>

# On a function: each parameter, by name, comes to hold the values of the parameters listed for it.
# Without it, a parameter taken by `&` keeps every other one, and nothing else is kept. On one with a
# body, it stands for the body, which calls code the analysis can't follow (the prelude's resolvers,
# deciding through the closures they hold): callers read the function as a native.
struct Keeps { into: Map<String, Array<String>> }

# Keeps what `it` is given until the holder is released, then releases it all, last first (§16):
# a disposable kept is disposed then, a future cancelled.
shared struct Holder<M in Mark>:
  kept: Array<Fn<(), Unit, M>>

# A holder keeping nothing yet; where it is declared decides how long what it keeps lives.
M fn holder<M in Mark>() -> Holder<M>: new Holder<M>{kept: []}

# Calls `make` now and keeps its value, of any type, as long as the holder lives: `hold.it start(job)`.
M fn it<T, M in Mark>(self: Holder<M>, make: Fn<(), T, M>):
  const value = make()
  self->kept.push(fn(): {
    const kept = value
  })
